  "id": "Compound::DB00519 - Gene::6564",
  "verbalization": "Trandolapril, a clinically used antihypertensive drug, is shown to bind to Slc15a2, a protein involved in transport processes across cellular membranes. Slc15a2 participates in multiple transport activities, including proton-dependent peptide transport and proton/oligonucleotide cotransport, which are all mediated by the same protein. These activities are linked to Slc15a1, suggesting a functional relationship between Slc15a2 and Slc15a1 in the context of membrane transport. The evidence suggests that Trandolapril may influence Slc15a1 through its interaction with Slc15a2, potentially modulating its transport activity. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Trandolapril in regulating membrane transport processes via Slc15a2, which in turn affects Slc15a1. The mechanism is supported by the shared participation of Slc15a2 and Slc15a1 in transport activities, indicating a functional dependency or co-regulation between these proteins.",
